  Speaking of tinkering with the language, can someone explain the
rationale behind not allowing CAhA to be negated with nai?  I'm sure
there is one, but why shouldn't (for example) "mi ka'e nai sanga" be
roughly equivalent to "mi na'e kakne lo nu sanga"?  (admittedly
pu'inai and nu'onai wouldn't be as useful as ka'enai and ca'anai)
         --gejyspa

